      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1430.137062817457,
      "y": 1516.8434851770664,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 1640.625,
      "height": 264,
      "seed": 114015905,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788715549432,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "text": "How do we scale to millions of connections from our users?\n- To scale to millions of connections, we'll horizontally scale the Document Service. \nWe can probably support a million+ connections per host, \nso we'll likely need O(100) for this application\n- Zookeper connects us to the right service based on DocumentID\n\nHow can we optimize document storage?\n- How can we optimize document storage?\n- First, we can evict documents from memory when the last user disconnects - we only need to keep documents in memory when users are active.\n- Second, we can compact documents by merging a string of edits into a single operation\n- Finally, we can periodically take dormant documents and write their operations out to S3. ",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do we scale to millions of connections from our users?\n- To scale to millions of connections, we'll horizontally scale the Document Service. \nWe can probably support a million+ connections per host, \nso we'll likely need O(100) for this application\n- Zookeper connects us to the right service based on DocumentID\n\nHow can we optimize document storage?\n- How can we optimize document storage?\n- First, we can evict documents from memory when the last user disconnects - we only need to keep documents in memory when users are active.\n- Second, we can compact documents by merging a string of edits into a single operation\n- Finally, we can periodically take dormant documents and write their operations out to S3. ",
      "lineHeight": 1.2,
      "baseline": 259
    }
  ],
